sections.body:
Instead of a plain value, you can pass the setter a *function*. React calls it with the latest pending value for that state and uses what it returns as the new value:

```jsx
setCount((c) => c + 1);
```

This matters when you call the setter for the same state more than once in a single event handler, before a re-render happens in between:

```jsx
function handleClick() {
  setCount(count + 1);
  setCount(count + 1);
}
```

Both calls read the same `count` from this render's closure (say, 0), so both mean "set state to 1". React doesn't treat them as two "add one" operations; after handleClick finishes the count is 1, not 2, however many times you repeat `count + 1`. Functional updates fix this because each one operates on the *result of the previous queued update*, not on the render-time snapshot:

```jsx
function handleClick() {
  setCount((c) => c + 1);
  setCount((c) => c + 1);
}
```

The first functional update receives 0 and produces 1; the second receives that 1 (not the render-time 0) and produces 2. This answers the common question 'why doesn't one click increment by 2?', and it's why functional updates exist rather than being a stylistic alternative. Updater functions should be pure: React may call them twice in development (Strict Mode) to catch side effects. Also note that since React 18, updates are batched automatically: several setter calls in one event handler (or in a timeout, promise or other callback) produce a single re-render, not one per call.

```jsx
function DelayedCount() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  function handleLogLater() {
    setTimeout(() => {
      console.log("count is", count);
    }, 2000);
  }

  function increment() {
    setCount(count + 1);
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={increment}>+1</button>
      <button onClick={handleLogLater}>Log in 2s</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

Suppose a user clicks "+1" once (count becomes 1 after the re-render), then clicks "Log in 2s" (the callback closes over `count` as it is *right now*, 1), then clicks "+1" twice more within the next second (count becomes 3). Two seconds after "Log in 2s", the console prints `count is 1`, not 3. The scheduled callback is tied to the render where it was created; the two later clicks each produce a new render with its own `increment` and its own `count`, but none of that changes the callback already waiting in the timer queue. Meanwhile the screen has shown the latest count the whole time (if you render it, as the next example does). Only the closure inside the timeout is out of date, not the component's state.

When the stale value is used to *compute new state* (not just read for display or logging), a functional update fixes it, because the update is applied to the latest queued value, not the one closed over when the callback was created:

```jsx
setTimeout(() => {
  setCount((c) => c + 1); // always increments from the current value
}, 2000);
```

But functional updates only help when you're *producing a new state value*. They don't help when you need to read the latest value from inside a stale closure without setting anything (for a log statement, a comparison, or passing it to another API). They're for computing a new value from the current one, not a general 'give me the live value' mechanism. For the read-only case, the standard escape hatch is a **ref** kept in sync with the state (covered in the next concept), since a ref's `.current` is read at call time through the same persistent object instead of a snapshot captured when the closure was created.

