sections.body:
```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  // effect body
}, [dep1, dep2]);
```

On every render, React compares each dependency to its value on the *previous* render (using `Object.is`); the effect re-runs only if at least one changed. The array must list every reactive value (state, props, or anything derived from them) that the effect body reads. Leaving one out is not a harmless optimization. It's a stale-closure bug of the kind covered for useState: the effect function is a closure created on the render that last caused it to run, and if a value it reads isn't listed, the effect has no reason to run again when that value changes, so it keeps using the old value from its last run.

That's what the `exhaustive-deps` rule in `eslint-plugin-react-hooks` flags: hard-to-notice bugs (an effect using stale data), not a style preference. Silencing the warning with an eslint-disable comment hides it while the bug remains. If an effect needs to read the latest value of something without re-running when it changes, React 19.2 adds `useEffectEvent` for that case. Two special cases: an empty array `[]` means 'run after the first render and not again' (in development, Strict Mode runs the effect, its cleanup and the effect again once on mount, to check that your cleanup works); omitting the array means 'run after every render', which is rarely intended.

If the function passed to `useEffect` returns another function, React treats that returned function as **cleanup**. It runs right before the effect runs again (for a newer set of dependency values) and once more when the component unmounts entirely:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  function handleResize() {
    setWidth(window.innerWidth);
  }
  window.addEventListener("resize", handleResize);

  return () => {
    window.removeEventListener("resize", handleResize);
  };
}, []);
```

Cleanup matters for anything that would otherwise leak or duplicate. A subscription that's never removed keeps invoking a callback tied to a component that may have re-run its effect or unmounted, calling stale closures or updating state for an instance that's gone. An interval that's never cleared keeps firing. And an effect that re-runs without cleanup in between (because its dependencies changed) accumulates subscriptions or timers instead of having one active at a time, so a resize handler that should fire once per resize fires once per leftover listener.

```jsx
function BuggyCounter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    setCount(count + 1);
  }, [count]);

  return <p>{count}</p>;
}
```

This effect depends on `count` and sets a new `count` on every run. Trace it: render (count = 0), effect runs on mount, setCount(1), re-render (count = 1), count changed so the effect runs again, setCount(2), re-render (count = 2), and so on, forever. The effect changes the very value that makes it run again. In development React warns about 'Maximum update depth exceeded' when it detects this kind of loop.

The fix is one of a few things: don't depend on a value you unconditionally overwrite inside the same effect (a functional update gives you the *previous* value without listing it as a dependency); add a conditional guard so the effect only calls setState when something relevant changed; or, often the right call, realize the logic doesn't need an effect. A lot of 'derived state' (a value computed from other state or props) doesn't belong in a useEffect-plus-setState pair; calculate it in the component body during render, with no effect, no extra state variable and no loop. The same goes for responding to a user action: if something should happen because the user clicked, do it in the click handler, not in an effect that watches for the resulting state change.

```jsx
function UserProfile({ userId }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    let isCurrent = true;

    fetchUser(userId).then((data) => {
      if (isCurrent) setUser(data);
    });

    return () => {
      isCurrent = false;
    };
  }, [userId]);

  return user ? <p>{user.name}</p> : <p>Loading...</p>;
}
```

This effect lists `userId` as its only dependency, since that's the only reactive value it reads (`setUser` is stable and needn't be listed), and re-fetches whenever it changes. The cleanup doesn't cancel the network request (use an `AbortController` and pass its signal to `fetch` if you want that), but it flips a flag so that if `userId` changes *before* the current fetch resolves (a race: the user clicks between two profiles quickly), the stale response is ignored instead of being applied for a userId that's no longer current. A dependency array matching what's read, plus a cleanup guarding against a late-arriving result, is the shape of most data-fetching effects. Hand-written fetching effects still miss caching, deduplication and retries, which is why many projects use a data-fetching library or a framework's loader instead. (`fetchUser` is a stand-in for your own API function.)

