sections.body:
```jsx
function AutoFocusInput() {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    inputRef.current.focus();
  }, []);

  return <input ref={inputRef} placeholder="Focused automatically" />;
}
```

Attaching `ref={inputRef}` to a JSX element tells React to set `inputRef.current` to the browser DOM node once it exists (right after that render is committed). It's an escape hatch to imperative DOM APIs that JSX has no declarative prop for: `.focus()`, `.scrollIntoView()`, measuring with `.getBoundingClientRect()`, or integrating a non-React library that needs a raw DOM node. `inputRef.current` is `null` before the element exists and becomes the node once React has mounted it, which is why the `.focus()` call above sits in a useEffect: effects run after the DOM has been updated for that render, so the node exists by then. In React 19, function components receive `ref` as an ordinary prop (no `forwardRef` needed), and a ref callback may return a cleanup function.

