sections.body:
A component function runs again from the top on every render. Any object literal, array literal or function written inside that body is therefore a new value each time, even when it looks identical to the previous render's:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const config = { color: "blue" }; // a new object on every render
  return <Child config={config} />;
}
```

`config` is a different reference on every render of Parent, even though `{ color: "blue" }` reads the same each time. That matters wherever something downstream cares about identity rather than content: a dependency array (which compares with `Object.is`) or a component wrapped in `React.memo` (which shallow-compares props the same way).

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback((id) => {
  doSomething(id);
}, [doSomething]);
```

`useCallback(fn, deps)` behaves like `useMemo(() => fn, deps)`. It's a dedicated name for the common case of memoizing a *function reference*, not a different mechanism, and anything it does, useMemo could also express.

examples.code:
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ onItemClick }) {
  console.log("ExpensiveList rendered");
  return <div>{/* imagine thousands of rows here */}</div>;
});

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleItemClick = useCallback((id) => {
    console.log("clicked", id);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Re-render Parent ({count})</button>
      <ExpensiveList onItemClick={handleItemClick} />
    </div>
  );
}

