Consider a `theme` value needed by a deeply nested `ThemedButton`, several layers below the component that actually owns it:

```jsx
function Page({ theme }) {
  return <Sidebar theme={theme} />;
}
function Sidebar({ theme }) {
  return <Panel theme={theme} />;
}
function Panel({ theme }) {
  return <ThemedButton theme={theme} />;
}
function ThemedButton({ theme }) {
  return <button className={theme}>Click</button>;
}
```

`Sidebar` and `Panel` both accept a `theme` prop and immediately forward it so it can reach `ThemedButton`; neither uses it. This is **prop drilling**, and it gets worse as the tree grows: every intermediate component's props become coupled to something that is really global configuration, and adding a consumer deep in the tree, or restructuring the tree, means touching every layer in between.

```jsx
const ThemeContext = createContext("light");

function Page() {
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">
      <Sidebar />
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
}
function Sidebar() {
  return <Panel />;
}
function Panel() {
  return <ThemedButton />;
}
function ThemedButton() {
  const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);
  return <button className={theme}>Click</button>;
}
```

`createContext(defaultValue)` creates a Context object. Wrapping part of the tree in `<ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">` makes that value available to any descendant, however deeply nested. `useContext(ThemeContext)`, called in any descendant, reads the value from the nearest enclosing Provider above it, and the default (`"light"`) is used only when there is no Provider above. `Sidebar` and `Panel` no longer mention theme, so they're no longer coupled to a value they never used. In React 19 you can write `<ThemeContext value="dark">` without `.Provider` (`.Provider` still works), and the new `use(ThemeContext)` API reads context too, including after a conditional or early return, which `useContext` can't do.

```jsx
const AppContext = createContext(null);

function App() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: "Amara" });
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState("light");
  const value = { user, theme, setUser, setTheme };

  return (
    <AppContext.Provider value={value}>
      <ThemeLabel />
      <UserGreeting />
    </AppContext.Provider>
  );
}

const ThemeLabel = memo(function ThemeLabel() {
  const { theme } = useContext(AppContext);
  return <span>{theme}</span>;
});
const UserGreeting = memo(function UserGreeting() {
  const { user } = useContext(AppContext);
  return <span>Hi, {user.name}</span>;
});
```

Calling `setTheme` here re-renders `App`, which builds a new `value` object, and because `AppContext`'s value reference changed, **every** component that calls `useContext(AppContext)` re-renders, including `UserGreeting`, even though `user` never changed. `useContext` subscribes a component to the context's value as a whole; React can't know that `UserGreeting` reads only `user`, and tracks only whether the value reference changed. (The consumers are wrapped in `memo` so the demonstration is clean. Without it, they would re-render whenever `App` does, simply because a parent's re-render re-renders its children, and that would hide the context effect. A context change re-renders consumers even through `memo`.)

